\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal C=\{(a,b)\mid a<b,\ a,b\in\mathbb Q\}$. Since every real number lies in some interval $(a,b)$ with rational endpoints, $\bigcup\mathcal C=\mathbb R$. If $(a,b),(c,d)\in\mathcal C$, then their intersection is empty or $(\max\{a,c\},\min\{b,d\})$, which again belongs to $\mathcal C$ whenever nonempty. Thus $\mathcal C$ is a basis for a topology on $\mathbb R$.

The lower limit topology has each interval $[r,s)$ as a basis element. Choose $r$ irrational and $s>r$. We claim that $[r,s)$ is not a union of elements of $\mathcal C$. If it were, some $(a,b)\in\mathcal C$ would contain $r$, since $r\in[r,s)$. But then $a<r$, so $a\in(a,b)$ while $a\notin[r,s)$, a contradiction. Hence $[r,s)$ is not open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$, although it is open in the lower limit topology. Therefore the two topologies are different; indeed, the topology generated by $\mathcal C$ is strictly coarser than the lower limit topology.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Set $\mathcal C=\{(a,b):a,b\in\mathbb Q,
<b\}$. The union of the members of $\mathcal C$ is $\mathbb R$, because between any real number and a sufficiently distant real number there are rational endpoints enclosing it. Moreover, the intersection of two members is either empty or an interval $(\max(a,c),\min(b,d))$ with rational endpoints. Thus $\mathcal C$ is a basis.

Let $r$ be irrational and let $s>r$. The set $[r,s)$ is open in the lower limit topology. It cannot be open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$: if $[r,s)=\bigcup_i(a_i,b_i)$ with rational $a_i<b_i$, then $r$ belongs to some $(a_i,b_i)$. Consequently $a_i<r$, and hence $a_i\in(a_i,b_i)\subseteq[r,s)$, contradicting $a_i<r$. Therefore $[r,s)$ is not open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$, so that topology differs from the lower limit topology. Since every $(a,b)$ is a lower-limit-open set, it is in fact strictly coarser.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Define $\mathcal C=\{(a,b)\mid a<b,\ a,b\in\mathbb Q\}$. For every $x\in\mathbb R$, choose rationals $a<x<b$; hence the union of the elements of $\mathcal C$ is $\mathbb R$. Given $(a,b),(c,d)\in\mathcal C$, their intersection is either empty or $(\max(a,c),\min(b,d))$, and in the latter case its endpoints are rational. Thus $\mathcal C$ satisfies the basis axioms and generates a topology.

Choose an irrational $r$ and any $s>r$. The interval $[r,s)$ is a basis element, and therefore an open set, in the lower limit topology. Suppose it were open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$. Then it would be a union of members of $\mathcal C$, so some $(a,b)\in\mathcal C$ containing $r$ would occur in that union. Since $a<r$ and $a\in(a,b)$, this interval contains a point $a<r$, which is impossible because $(a,b)\subseteq[r,s)$. Thus $[r,s)$ is not open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$. The generated topology is therefore different from, and strictly coarser than, the lower limit topology.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal C=\{(a,b)\mid a<b,\ a,b\in\mathbb Q\}$. It covers $\mathbb R$: for each $x\in\mathbb R$, density of $\mathbb Q$ gives rationals $a<x<b$. If $(a,b),(c,d)\in\mathcal C$, then their intersection is either empty or $(\max\{a,c\},\min\{b,d\})$, whose endpoints are rational. Hence $\mathcal C$ is a basis and generates a topology on $\mathbb R$.

Every member $(a,b)$ of $\mathcal C$ is open in the lower limit topology, since

a\in(a,b)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad (a,b)=\bigcup_{x\in(a,b)}[x,b),

so the topology generated by $\mathcal C$ is contained in the lower limit topology. To prove the containment is strict, choose irrational $r$ and $s>r$. The set $[r,s)$ is open in the lower limit topology. If it were open in the topology generated by $\mathcal C$, it would be a union of rational-endpoint open intervals. One such interval $(a,b)$ would contain $r$, forcing $a<r$; but then $a\in(a,b)\subseteq[r,s)$, a contradiction. Thus $[r,s)$ is not open in the generated topology. Therefore the collection is a basis generating a topology different from, indeed strictly coarser than, the lower limit topology.
\end{proof}
